# `#562` (armmsrmodekill): one privileged guest `MSR` naming a mode this model does not bank -- or an SPSR restore whose SPSR names one, including an SPSR never written -- ended the emulator; the mode field is declined now and announced once per site, defended by a 47-row detector whose review chain ran five repairs

**The defect, witnessed at rung 3.**  `arm_load_register_bank()` and `arm_save_register_bank()` in
`src/cpus/cpu_arm.c` switch on the seven mode values this model banks (USR 10, FIQ 11, IRQ 12, SVC 13, ABT 17, UND 1b,
SYS 1f -- six banks, USR and SYS share one), and their `default:` arms called `fatal(); exit(1);`.  Three guest
instructions write CPSR's mode field: `msr_imm` (`MSR CPSR_c,#0x14` = 0xE321F014), `arm_pop`'s `LDM {..pc}^` SPSR
restore, and the data-processing S-to-PC restore (`MOVS pc,lr`).  Any of them naming an unmodelled mode reached Load's
`default:` and ended the process -- and **no MSR is needed**: `cpu_new()` zero-fills the CPU and nothing initialises
the SPSRs, so the first `MOVS pc,lr` from the boot SVC32 before any exception has written `spsr_svc` loads mode 0x00
and dies (grok, pass 1).  `regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` on the unmodified `-E testarm`, HEAD `ef577140`: the 17-row
witness scored 14/17 -- `kill 14`, `kill 15` and `save 2s` dead, every control green
(`artifacts/armmsrmodekill/witness_20261007/run_head.out`); the shipped 47-row detector scores 18/47 on HEAD, 29
