regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_f2_20261007\codex.txt:2647:7 | RIGHT: **The rejection is permissible; the warrant is false.** DDI0100I says a reserved mode makes the instruction’s results UNPREDICTABLE, without guaranteeing every other field’s effect. Whole-operation decline buys preservation of flags and interrupt masks, and—if implemented sufficiently early—avoids the return’s PC, register-load and writeback effects. Field-only deliberately preserves those effects. Neither policy is architecturally mandatory. Your proposed I/F readback would distinguish them; the supplied measurements do not yet do so. [Local architecture text](C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/_scratchpad/ddi0100i.txt:9691).
regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_f2_20261007\codex.txt:2651:9 | RIGHT: Shared classification outside the repeated DPI template is appropriate. However, copying seven labels into another switch does not mechanically establish one source of truth, and the proposed `static` linkage is wrong.
regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_f2_20261007\codex.txt:2655:11 | WRONG: **The debugger mechanism and both-backstops reachability claim are wrong.** `put` writes memory: it evaluates its destination as an address and performs a store. Register/settings writes use the debugger’s assignment mechanism. Moreover, after the completed sketch, a transition from an invalid current mode to a valid destination exercises Save’s fallback and then a valid Load. With conditional DPI banking, none of the four callers demonstrates debugger-only reachability of Load’s default. Retain that fallback defensively, but row (d) cannot substantiate both backstops. [Debugger memory write](C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/debugger/debugger_cmds.c:1413), [settings assignment](C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/debugger/debugger.c:207).
