regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2_20261007\codex.txt:1678:tools/CLAUDE.md:783:| agy 3.8 (high) | `agy --dangerously-skip-permissions --add-dir <dir> --print-timeout 30m --model gemini-3.8-flash-high --effort high --print "<short prompt pointing at brief file>"` — reads files via `--add-dir`; no stdin prompt mode. **Model set by user directive 2026-08-13.** PASS BOTH `--model` and `--effort`: the effort tier is baked into the model name (`-high`/`-medium`/`-low`) AND there is a separate `--effort` flag, so naming only one leaves the other at its default. Before this, `panel.sh` passed NEITHER while printing "agy(high)" — the banner was an overclaim. **`agy models` is the authority on valid names** (it lists gemini-3.7/3.6/3.5-flash and 3.1-pro tiers, plus claude-sonnet-4-6 and claude-opus-4-6-thinking); a wrong name is a SEAT FAILURE, so re-check after any CLI upgrade.  *** AND IT HAS A QUOTA.  MEASURED 2026-08-30, THE FIRST agy WALL ON RECORD, AND IT ARRIVED WHILE THE SEAT WAS BEING DELIBERATELY PUSHED. ***  `Error: Individual quota reached. Please upgrade your subscription to increase your limits. Resets in 1h56m37s.` -- **111 bytes, on stderr, with the process exiting immediately**, so the `.out` file is ZERO BYTES and nothing is written to the panel dir but that one line.  **THE ROSTER ASSUMPTION IT OVERTURNS:** the owner's own read was *"seems like there is limitless agy use"*, and this project had never seen it wall, so it was being farmed 3 jobs at a time.  Six substantial invocations inside about forty minutes reached the limit.  Treat it like Grok's paid cap: **not queryable, not unlimited, and a raised cap has the same failure mode further out.**  *** THE DETECTION IS EASY AND THE MISREADING IS NOT. ***  It states its own reset time, which no other seat here does -- so a walled agy tells you exactly when to re-fire, and the re-fire-a-walled-seat-once rule becomes a scheduled thing rather than a guess.  But **the zero-byte `.out` is the trap**: this file's own rule says a zero-byte agy log is the NORMAL mid-run state because agy does not stream.  Zero bytes plus a live process is mid-run; zero bytes plus 111 bytes on stderr is a WALL.  **Check the `.err`, not the size** -- `panel.sh` writes one per seat and this is what it is for.  *Recorded because a seat that walls with the machine otherwise healthy is the state this project most often misfiles: three jobs failed identically and instantly, which reads like a launcher bug and is not.* |
regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2_20261007\codex.txt:1680:tools/CLAUDE.md:845:| Grok 4.7 (xhigh) -- *** OWNER DIRECTIVE 2026-09-21: "Make sure to use Grok 4.7 extra high".  VERIFIED BEFORE THE EDIT by running `grok models`, which now lists FOUR: **grok-4.7 (default)**, grok-4.7-build-fast, grok-4.6, grok-4.5 -- so the "lists TWO" measurement below is 2026-08-26's and is superseded by this line, not by argument.  Then VERIFIED LIVE: a `--prompt-file` invocation at `-m grok-4.7 --effort xhigh` returned BOTH liveness tokens (the nonce and the first line of CHECKPOINT.md, verbatim) and exited 0, and the bogus-value discriminator still holds on 4.7 (`--effort ultrahigh` -> `unknown effort level 'ultrahigh'; use one of: xhigh, high, medium, low`), so xhigh is a real tier and the top of it.  `panel.sh` carries `GROK_MODEL="grok-4.7"`. *** | `grok --prompt-file <brief> -m grok-4.7 --effort xhigh --permission-mode plan --cwd <proj> < /dev/null` — **xhigh set 2026-08-13 by user directive and VERIFIED to be a real tier, not assumed: passing a bogus value makes the CLI name its own list, `use one of: xhigh, high, medium, low`, which proves the flag is validated AND that xhigh is the top of it.** That discriminator is the general technique — if a bad value were silently ignored, acceptance would prove nothing and the seat would quietly run at the default, which is precisely the trap the agy seat sat in. **A bad effort value EXITS 0 while printing its error**, so exit status cannot catch it; `panel.sh`'s seat check greps for "unknown effort level"/"unknown model" alongside the quota patterns. — binary at `~/.grok/bin/grok.exe`, **added 2026-08-13 by user directive.** *** `--prompt-file` TAKES THE BRIEF FROM A FILE, so the ~32 KB Windows argv cap DOES NOT APPLY — this is the only CLI seat immune to the failure that killed Codex on a 36 KB brief today, so it is the seat to trust with a large inlined diff. *** `--permission-mode plan` keeps it READ-ONLY (verified: it read a repo file and answered correctly, without prompting or hanging) and `--cwd` lets it consult source like Codex and agy do. `grok models` is the authority on IDs. **MEASURED 2026-08-26 by running it: it lists TWO** -- `grok-4.6` (default) and `grok-4.5`. *** THIS CELL CARRIED BOTH "today it lists exactly one" AND "now lists TWO" AT ONCE, two present-tense claims that cannot both be true, until the exemplar audit found them. Settled by running the command rather than by choosing between them. *** **AND THE SAME RUN REFUTED THE TIER-CHECK CLAIM, which mattered more than the count.** This said the model list is "itself a cheap tier check" for whether the subscription is active. It is not: `grok models` printed **`You are not authenticated.`** and STILL listed both models -- so the list is served without auth and proves nothing about tier. **The seat was verified live in the same minute by a real invocation** (`--prompt-file`, xhigh, plan mode: it answered, read `CHECKPOINT.md`, and exited 0). **So the ONLY trustworthy health test is firing the seat.** That is the rule this file already states for walled seats -- *do not reason about whether a seat is back, test it* -- and the tier-check shortcut was a way of dodging it. The paid cap is still not queryable (no `usage`/`quota` subcommand; `du` is disk usage), so DO NOT ASSUME IT IS UNLIMITED — a raised cap has the same failure mode further out. `panel.sh`'s seat check scans any non-answering seat for quota/rate/auth text generically, so a wall is reported as an OPERATIONAL failure rather than counted as agreement. **FREE-TIER HISTORY, kept because it calibrates expectations: the free tier died after ~4 substantial invocations, mid-way through its first real review — and even so it found a genuine defect in that partial output (three surviving "un-fakeable" overclaims that two other seats had missed).** |
regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/panel_pass2_20261007\codex.txt:1681:tools/CLAUDE.md:846:| Ollama cloud ×3 | POST `http://localhost:11434/api/generate` from **git-bash** python (WSL cannot reach the host API); write responses to utf-8 files, never `print()` (cp1252 crash). Three seats, verified live 2026-08-10: **`glm-5.3:cloud`** — *** MIGRATED TWICE BY OWNER DIRECTIVE: glm-5.2 -> glm-5.3-flash on 2026-08-28, then glm-5.3-flash -> **glm-5.3** on 2026-08-29. BOTH VERIFIED BEFORE THE EDIT, which is the discipline that matters here: `ollama pull glm-5.3:cloud` succeeded and an `api/generate` call returned the liveness token EXACTLY (`done_reason=stop`, eval_count 52, no thinking-burn). *** Verified BEFORE the edit rather than after: `ollama pull glm-5.3-flash:cloud` succeeded while the near-miss `glm-5.3flash:cloud` FAILED with `file does not exist`, and an `api/generate` call returned its liveness nonce exactly (`done_reason=stop`, 25 bytes, no thinking-burn). **The tag lives in TWO files and both were changed together** — `panel.sh`'s `OLLAMA1` and `adjudicate.sh`'s two loops, where the second loop must carry the `${m%%:*}` short form or the seat check hunts a filename the run never writes and reports a LIVE seat as missing. **The seat itself is keyed by FILENAME (`ollama_glm.txt`) everywhere else**, so `check_seats_read.py` and `seat_verdicts.py` needed no edit — record a seat by its transport, not its tag —, **`deepseek-v4-pro:cloud`** (DeepSeek is back — v3.2 was retired 2026-07-15 but v4-pro is live; the exact tag needs `-pro`), **`minimax-m3:cloud`**. All three are THINKING models — send NO `num_predict` cap or hidden reasoning eats the budget and the response is empty. Recheck: `curl -s localhost:11434/api/tags`. |
