affects how much \"booking\" the filing needs. Single fact: readers of re_linear_a.)\n\nAlso the debugger-tap recommendation (one sentence, as follow-up row, not shipped).\n\nLength: substantial but tight. The instruction says \"Do not pad. A short answer that names one real defect beats a long one that restates the brief.\" I'll aim for something like 700\u20131000 words, dense, no restating. Maybe a bit more given four questions demanded answers. Let me draft.\n\nVerdict line: \"VERDICT: Ship B \u2014 the `>> 3` \u2192 `>> 1` shift plus comment \u2014 but only with the created over-run affirmatively booked (its own filed instance, not silent inheritance); reject A's conjunct (unwitnessable under the project's own oracle rules, and the wrong shape for a tap-level class), reject C (contradicts the standing prerequisite ruling). One premise everything hangs on is NOT in the packet: the numeric value of CRIME_RE_TEX (see P0).\"\n\nThen sections. Label READ/REASONED.\n\nP0 (premise audit): The index math in the agreed facts presupposes `(relative_addr & 0xff)` == in-window offset, i.e., CRIME_RE_TEX \u2261 0 (mod 256). READ: the code masks with `& 0xff` and the window is checked against the raw macro. If the macro is not 256-aligned, premise 2's \">>1 is bounds-safe under 8-alignment\" is FALSE \u2014 max `(0xff)>>1 = 127`, writes to 130, OOB even on guest-aligned stores \u2014 and the one-token fix is wrong; the correct edit is `(relative_addr - CRIME_RE_TEX) >> 1`. Since all three positions and all five agreed facts inherit this assumption, it is the packet's largest unhedged premise. Not settleable from packet; settling fact: the macro's value (or an in-packet statement that `relative_addr & 0xff` equals the in-window offset). Everything below is conditional on 256-alignment.\n\nAlso note (minor, READ-consistent): premise 2's \"indices 27..30 at every in-window offset\" is worst-case phrasing (at offset 0 the indices are 0..3); the intended claim \u2014 max index 30